{"id": "SATM2MZGDCW", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 11)$ and $(5, 23)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 4x + 3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 4x - 3$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = 3x + 5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = \\frac{3}{4}x + 11$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{23 - 11}{5 - 2} = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$. Using point $(2, 11)$: $11 = 4(2) + b$, so $b = 3$. The equation is $y = 4x + 3$. The equation $y = 4x - 3$ has the correct slope but an incorrect sign on the $y$-intercept, resulting from subtracting $b$ instead of solving for it properly. The equation $y = 3x + 5$ reverses the rise and run in the slope calculation, getting $\\frac{3}{12}$ inverted to $3$, then adjusting $b$ accordingly. The equation $y = \\frac{3}{4}x + 11$ uses the inverted slope $\\frac{3}{4}$ and incorrectly sets the $y$-intercept equal to the $y$-coordinate of the first point."}
{"id": "SATM2N6105H", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "Which of the following ordered pairs $(x, y)$ satisfies the equation $5x + 3y = 41$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$(2, 9)$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$(4, 7)$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$(5, 6)$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$(7, 3)$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 4$ and $y = 7$: $5(4) + 3(7) = 20 + 21 = 41$, which matches the right side. For $(2, 9)$: $5(2) + 3(9) = 10 + 27 = 37 \\neq 41$. For $(5, 6)$: $5(5) + 3(6) = 25 + 18 = 43 \\neq 41$. For $(7, 3)$: $5(7) + 3(3) = 35 + 9 = 44 \\neq 41$. Only $(4, 7)$ produces the required sum of $41$."}
{"id": "SATM2N68Q60", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $m$ has the equation $5x + 13y = 52$, and line $n$ is perpendicular to line $m$ and passes through the point $(-2, 7)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $n$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{9}{13}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{9}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{61}{13}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{61}{5}$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "Rewriting line $m$ in slope-intercept form: $13y = -5x + 52$, so the slope of $m$ is $-\\frac{5}{13}$. The perpendicular slope is $\\frac{13}{5}$. Using point-slope form with $(-2, 7)$: $y - 7 = \\frac{13}{5}(x + 2)$, giving $y = \\frac{13}{5}x + \\frac{26}{5} + 7 = \\frac{13}{5}x + \\frac{26}{5} + \\frac{35}{5} = \\frac{13}{5}x + \\frac{61}{5}$. The $y$-intercept is $\\frac{61}{5}$. The value $\\frac{9}{5}$ results from a sign error, using $(x - 2)$ instead of $(x + 2)$, yielding $7 - \\frac{26}{5} = \\frac{9}{5}$. The value $\\frac{61}{13}$ results from using the original slope $-\\frac{5}{13}$ instead of the perpendicular slope. The value $\\frac{9}{13}$ combines both errors."}
{"id": "SATM2N79EU2", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line in the $xy$-plane has the equation $5x + 9y = 45$. What is the $x$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$9$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-9$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{5}{9}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The $x$-intercept occurs when $y = 0$. Substituting: $5x = 45$, so $x = 9$. The value $5$ results from setting $x = 0$ instead and solving for $y$ ($9y = 45$, $y = 5$), which gives the $y$-intercept rather than the $x$-intercept. The value $-9$ incorrectly applies a negative sign to the correct answer. The value $\\frac{5}{9}$ confuses the slope of the line (in slope-intercept form, $y = -\\frac{5}{9}x + 5$, the slope is $-\\frac{5}{9}$) with the intercept."}
{"id": "SATM2N7SPMF", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line is perpendicular to the line $3x + 8y = 24$ and passes through the point $(5, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{49}{3}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{40}{3}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{9}{8}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{31}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewrite $3x + 8y = 24$ in slope-intercept form: $y = -\\frac{3}{8}x + 3$, so its slope is $-\\frac{3}{8}$. The perpendicular slope is the negative reciprocal: $\\frac{8}{3}$. Using point-slope form with $(5, -3)$: $y + 3 = \\frac{8}{3}(x - 5)$. At $x = 0$: $y + 3 = -\\frac{40}{3}$, so $y = -\\frac{40}{3} - 3 = -\\frac{40}{3} - \\frac{9}{3} = -\\frac{49}{3}$. The distractor $-\\frac{40}{3}$ results from stopping at $y + 3 = -\\frac{40}{3}$ and reporting $-\\frac{40}{3}$ without subtracting the $3$. The distractor $-\\frac{9}{8}$ comes from using the original slope $-\\frac{3}{8}$ instead of the perpendicular slope, yielding $y + 3 = -\\frac{3}{8}(0 - 5) = \\frac{15}{8}$ and $y = \\frac{15}{8} - 3 = -\\frac{9}{8}$. The distractor $\\frac{31}{3}$ arises from using slope $-\\frac{8}{3}$ (wrong sign on the reciprocal), giving $y + 3 = -\\frac{8}{3}(0 - 5) = \\frac{40}{3}$ and $y = \\frac{40}{3} - 3 = \\frac{31}{3}$."}
