{"id": "SATM2NTB98M", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line is perpendicular to the line $5x - 2y = 13$ and passes through the point $(4, -3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-13$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{23}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{7}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{23}{5}$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Rewriting $5x - 2y = 13$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - \\frac{13}{2}$, so the original slope is $\\frac{5}{2}$. The perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{2}{5}$. Using point-slope form: $y - (-3) = -\\frac{2}{5}(x - 4)$, so $y + 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{8}{5}$. Solving gives $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + \\frac{8}{5} - \\frac{15}{5} = -\\frac{2}{5}x - \\frac{7}{5}$, and the $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{7}{5}$. The value $-13$ results from using the parallel slope $\\frac{5}{2}$ instead of the perpendicular slope. The value $-\\frac{23}{5}$ comes from using the reciprocal $\\frac{2}{5}$ without negating it. The value $\\frac{23}{5}$ results from a sign error in substituting the point $(4, -3)$, writing $y - 3$ instead of $y + 3$."}
{"id": "SATM2NY03WS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(3, -2)$ and $(9, 16)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "-11", "explanation": "First, find the slope: $m = \\frac{16 - (-2)}{9 - 3} = \\frac{18}{6} = 3$. Using point-slope form with $(3, -2)$: $y - (-2) = 3(x - 3)$, which gives $y + 2 = 3x - 9$, so $y = 3x - 11$. The $y$-intercept is $-11$."}
{"id": "SATM2O0XZ4J", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "What is the slope of the line that passes through the points $(-3, 14)$ and $(9, -22)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{1}{3}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{1}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$3$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope formula gives $m = \\frac{-22 - 14}{9 - (-3)} = \\frac{-36}{12} = -3$. The value $-\\frac{1}{3}$ results from inverting the fraction, computing the change in $x$ over the change in $y$ instead of rise over run. The value $\\frac{1}{3}$ compounds the inversion error with a sign error, losing the negative. The value $3$ results from dropping the negative sign from the correct calculation."}
{"id": "SATM2O20L27", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A line passes through the points $(2, 11)$ and $(5, 29)$. Which of the following is the equation of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = 6x - 1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = 6x + 1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -6x + 23$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{31}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{29 - 11}{5 - 2} = \\frac{18}{3} = 6$. Using point-slope form with the point $(2, 11)$: $y - 11 = 6(x - 2)$, which simplifies to $y = 6x - 12 + 11 = 6x - 1$. The equation $y = 6x + 1$ results from an arithmetic slip when computing $-12 + 11$, getting $+1$ instead of $-1$. The equation $y = -6x + 23$ uses a negated slope, a sign error, which when applied through $(2, 11)$ gives $y - 11 = -6(x - 2)$, yielding $y = -6x + 23$. The equation $y = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{31}{3}$ uses the reciprocal of the slope rather than the slope itself, reflecting a concept gap about the slope formula."}
