{"id": "SATM2OD610E", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A line passes through the point $(2, 11)$ and has a slope of $3$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-5$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$17$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Using $y = mx + b$: $11 = 3(2) + b$, so $11 = 6 + b$ and $b = 5$. The value $-5$ results from a sign error when subtracting $6$ from $11$. The value $6$ comes from computing $3 \\times 2 = 6$ and mistaking that product for the intercept. The value $17$ comes from adding $6$ to $11$ instead of subtracting."}
