{"id": "SATM2OFDFM4", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "Line $p$ has the equation $5x + 3y = 17$. Line $q$ is perpendicular to line $p$ and passes through the point $(4, -1)$. What is the equation of line $q$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{17}{5}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = -\\frac{5}{3}x + \\frac{17}{3}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = \\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{23}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{7}{5}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Rewriting line $p$: $3y = -5x + 17$, so $y = -\\frac{5}{3}x + \\frac{17}{3}$, giving slope $-\\frac{5}{3}$. The perpendicular slope is $\\frac{3}{5}$. Using point-slope with $(4, -1)$: $y + 1 = \\frac{3}{5}(x - 4)$, so $y = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{12}{5} - 1 = \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{17}{5}$. The option $y = -\\frac{5}{3}x + \\frac{17}{3}$ uses the original slope instead of the perpendicular reciprocal, a concept gap. The option $y = \\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{23}{3}$ uses the reciprocal without negating, a sign error. The option $y = -\\frac{3}{5}x + \\frac{7}{5}$ negates the perpendicular slope incorrectly, an equation setup error."}
{"id": "SATM2ONMQ4N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(1, 9)$ and $(4, -3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$y = -12x + 21$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$y = -4x - 13$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$y = -4x + 13$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$y = 4x + 5$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-3 - 9}{4 - 1} = \\frac{-12}{3} = -4$. Using point-slope form with $(1, 9)$: $y - 9 = -4(x - 1)$, so $y = -4x + 4 + 9 = -4x + 13$. The distractor $y = -12x + 21$ comes from using the rise $-12$ directly as the slope without dividing by the run, then computing $b = 9 - (-12)(1) = 21$. The distractor $y = -4x - 13$ results from a sign error computing the $y$-intercept as $4 - 9 = -13$ instead of $4 + 9 = 13$ when rearranging $y = -4x + (9 + 4)$. The distractor $y = 4x + 5$ arises from dropping the negative sign on the slope, computing $m = 4$, then $b = 9 - 4(1) = 5$."}
{"id": "SATM2OQGBVE", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The line $7x - 3y = 15$ is perpendicular to line $m$, and line $m$ passes through the point $(14, -1)$. What is the $y$-intercept of line $m$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$7$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Rewriting $7x - 3y = 15$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{7}{3}x - 5$, so its slope is $\\frac{7}{3}$. The perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{3}{7}$. Using point-slope form with $(14, -1)$: $y + 1 = -\\frac{3}{7}(x - 14)$, giving $y = -\\frac{3}{7}x + 6 - 1 = -\\frac{3}{7}x + 5$. The $y$-intercept is $5$. The value $-7$ results from using $\\frac{3}{7}$ (reciprocal without negating) as the perpendicular slope, which yields $y = \\frac{3}{7}x - 7$. The value $-5$ comes from reading the $y$-intercept of the original line. The value $7$ results from writing $y - 1$ instead of $y + 1$ in the point-slope equation, producing $y = -\\frac{3}{7}x + 7$."}
{"id": "SATM2OWTF4U", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A line in the $xy$-plane is perpendicular to the line $2x - 5y = 15$ and passes through the point $(10, 3)$. What is the $y$-intercept of this line?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-22$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$28$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "Rewriting $2x - 5y = 15$ in slope-intercept form gives $y = \\frac{2}{5}x - 3$, so the slope is $\\frac{2}{5}$. The perpendicular slope is the negative reciprocal: $-\\frac{5}{2}$. Using point-slope form with $(10, 3)$: $y - 3 = -\\frac{5}{2}(x - 10)$, so $y = -\\frac{5}{2}x + 25 + 3 = -\\frac{5}{2}x + 28$. The $y$-intercept is $28$. The value $-22$ results from using $\\frac{5}{2}$ (reciprocal without negating) as the slope: $y = \\frac{5}{2}x - 25 + 3 = \\frac{5}{2}x - 22$. The value $-1$ results from using the original slope $\\frac{2}{5}$ instead of the perpendicular slope: $y = \\frac{2}{5}x - 4 + 3 = \\frac{2}{5}x - 1$. The value $7$ results from negating the slope but not taking the reciprocal, using $-\\frac{2}{5}$: $y = -\\frac{2}{5}x + 4 + 3 = -\\frac{2}{5}x + 7$."}
