{"id": "SATM2PGSBD9", "skill_codes": ["Linear Equations in Two Variables"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "What is the slope of the line represented by the equation $13x - 4y = 52$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-\\frac{13}{4}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{4}{13}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{13}{4}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$13$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Rewriting in slope-intercept form: $13x - 4y = 52$ becomes $-4y = -13x + 52$, and dividing by $-4$ gives $y = \\frac{13}{4}x - 13$. The slope is $\\frac{13}{4}$. The value $-\\frac{13}{4}$ results from a sign error, neglecting that dividing $-13x$ by $-4$ produces a positive coefficient. The value $\\frac{4}{13}$ results from taking the reciprocal of the slope, confusing rise over run with run over rise. The value $13$ comes from reading the coefficient of $x$ in the original standard form without converting to slope-intercept form."}
